    </button>
  </header>

  <main id="top">
    <section class="wrap hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Five minds, many tabs</p>
        <h1 id="hero-title">We build with AI.<br><em>We still read</em> the diff.</h1>
        <p class="hero-copy">We’re five computer science students navigating the exciting, occasionally overconfident world of software engineering. Meet the humans behind the prompts, pull requests, and suspiciously long build waits.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-art" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="orbit"></div>
        <div class="sticker"><span>Human judgment, machine speed<small>Ship thoughtfully</small></span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap" aria-labelledby="team-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The people behind the pixels</p>
          <h2 id="team-title">Meet the team</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-note">Five perspectives. One shared dislike of mysterious bugs.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="team-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Mohammad.png}}" alt="Photo of Mohammad">
          <h3>Mohammad</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Mohammad is a first-semester computer science master’s student interested in full-stack development and gaming. He’ll patiently spend 40 hours on a story-driven game, though a 30-second test build can test his sanity. He sees AI as a quick way through boilerplate and syntax lookups, but warns that it can invent subtle logical flaws. To him, AI is an eager junior developer: helpful, fast, and in need of careful code review.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: He’ll sit through a 40-hour game with zero skips, but a 30-second build tests his sanity.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Nithya.png}}" alt="Photo of Nithya">
          <h3>Nithya</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 7th semester (Thesis stage)</p>
          <p class="bio">Nithya is a thesis-stage computer science student drawn to distributed systems and cloud architecture, with badminton and board games as welcome breaks. She has rewritten her thesis abstract more times than she has recompiled her code. She finds AI assistants productive for familiar tasks and boilerplate, but sees them falter in legacy systems and subtle concurrency problems. For Nithya, AI can be a fast copilot—but it still needs experienced oversight at the controls.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: She has rewritten her thesis abstract more times than she has recompiled her code.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI autocomplete hasn’t eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Rafee.png}}" alt="Photo of Rafee">
          <h3>Rafee</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Rafee is a first-semester computer science master’s student interested in frontend UI/UX and the React and Next.js ecosystem. Away from the screen, he plays table tennis and takes photographs. He judges coffee shops by Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub. As a newer developer, he values AI as an interactive tutor that can demystify compiler errors, while cautioning that accepting answers too early can create an illusion of understanding.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: He judges coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 If you can’t debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Raghvendra.png}}" alt="Photo of Raghvendra">
          <h3>Raghvendra</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 3rd semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Raghvendra is a third-semester computer science student focused on machine learning infrastructure and data engineering, with hiking and chess on the side. He’ll automate a five-minute task for four hours and call it an investment. He finds AI useful for rapid prototypes, SQL, and boilerplate endpoints, but not a replacement for architectural judgment. In his view, missing context and deprecated methods make AI a good friction-reducer—not a decision-maker.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: He’ll spend four hours automating a manual five-minute task and call it an investment.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 90% of current “AI agent” workflows are fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Sayemah.png}}" alt="Photo of Sayemah">
          <h3>Sayemah</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Sayemah is a first-semester computer science master’s student who enjoys calligraphy, cooking, travel, and human-computer interaction. She can master a traditional recipe on the first try, but a missing curly brace can ruin the evening. She sees AI as a way to make creative prototyping faster and lower the barrier to new ideas. Still, she believes software depends on communication and empathy as much as syntax, and that generated code can lack design intention.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: She can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace ruins her evening.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 Prompt engineering isn’t the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.</p>
        </article>
